Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.855 --> 00:00:06.835
Let's say that you and I decide to go out and get some lunch.

00:00:06.835 --> 00:00:09.545
I decide to get a burger with the works

00:00:09.545 --> 00:00:11.905
And you are a bit of an alcoholic, so you just decide to skip the food entirely

00:00:11.905 --> 00:00:14.805
And get 3 different kinds of beer

00:00:14.805 --> 00:00:18.085
Being the kind, sharing people that we are, we each offer the other a sample of our lunch

00:00:18.085 --> 00:00:21.145
Even though my burger tastes great and is very flavorful to me, you think it's bland

00:00:24.205 --> 00:00:27.425
And on the flip side, even though you got 3 very different types of beer

00:00:27.425 --> 00:00:30.675
They all taste the same and very bitter to me. Why is that the case?

00:00:30.675 --> 00:00:33.855
Why do different people taste things differently?

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:36.805
Let's start by looking at where taste comes from

00:00:36.805 --> 00:00:37.145
Let's start by looking at where taste comes from

00:00:37.145 --> 00:00:40.055
Taste, scientifically referred to as Gustatory Perception, or Gustation,

00:00:40.055 --> 00:00:42.145
Is the sensation we experience when a food or other substance in the mouth

00:00:42.145 --> 00:00:44.445
Is the sensation we experience when a food or other substance in the mouth

00:00:44.445 --> 00:00:47.005
Reacts chemically with what are called taste receptor cells

00:00:47.005 --> 00:00:50.075
These cells are located within your taste buds

00:00:50.075 --> 00:00:53.235
Which are mostly concentrated on your tongue and each contain 50-100 of these taste receptor cells

00:00:53.235 --> 00:00:55.975
You've probably heard that you can only taste certain flavors on certain portions of your tounge

00:00:55.975 --> 00:00:59.295
But that's not actually the case

00:00:59.645 --> 00:01:02.365
The section of the human brain responsible for perception of taste

00:01:02.365 --> 00:01:06.700
is called the Gustatory Cortex, which is located right by the areas that control chewing and swallowing

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:07.815
Now the human tongue, obviously with help from the brain

00:01:07.815 --> 00:01:10.555
Now the human tongue, obviously with help from the brain

00:01:10.555 --> 00:01:12.495
Has the ability to detect and differentiate between 5 separate elements of taste

00:01:12.495 --> 00:01:15.185
Which are broadly classified as salty, sour bitter, sweet, and umami, or savory

00:01:15.185 --> 00:01:18.225
Which are broadly classified as salty, sour bitter, sweet, and umami, or savory

00:01:18.225 --> 00:01:21.105
These aren't arbitrary catitigories

00:01:21.105 --> 00:01:24.115
Rather, they've been tested and confirmed to be distinct from one another

00:01:24.115 --> 00:01:26.245
Besides the 5 basic tastes,

00:01:26.245 --> 00:01:29.205
we all know that smell has a lot to do with perceived flavor as well.

00:01:29.205 --> 00:01:32.095
When you are sick or have a stuffy nose, food seems bland or tastless

00:01:32.095 --> 00:01:34.765
Other contributes to flavor include texture, temperature, and coolness or hotness, like a mint or chili pepper.

00:01:37.775 --> 00:01:40.655
This full assortment of sensations gives us the experience we know as taste.

00:01:40.655 --> 00:01:43.515
Evolutionary speaking,  taste played an important role in survival

00:01:46.195 --> 00:01:49.205
Bitterness in foods helped our ancestors learn to avoid potentially poisonous plants

00:01:49.205 --> 00:01:51.055
And sweetness helped identify energy rich foods

00:01:51.055 --> 00:01:54.215
Which is part of the reason we are drawn to unhealthy snacks and drinks today.

00:01:54.215 --> 00:01:57.235
That's basic taste in a nutshell

00:01:57.235 --> 00:02:00.075
But why do we experience foods differently?

00:02:00.075 --> 00:02:03.015
Why is something bitter to me but not someone else?

00:02:03.015 --> 00:02:06.065
The two main components of of individual taste perception are genetics and geography.

00:02:06.065 --> 00:02:09.035
Let's start with genetics.

00:02:09.035 --> 00:02:12.095
Between individuals, there can be considerable differences of the number and shape

00:02:12.095 --> 00:02:14.555
of fungiform papillae, the stuctures that house taste buds.

00:02:14.555 --> 00:02:17.575
The more of these structures you have, the more taste buds you have

00:02:17.575 --> 00:02:20.295
And the more taste buds you have,

00:02:20.295 --> 00:02:23.315
The stronger the signal sent from the mouth to your brain.

00:02:23.315 --> 00:02:26.485
These stronger signals cause people to experience flavors and often smells much more strongly.

00:02:26.485 --> 00:02:29.455
These people can often easily distinguish between individual flavors in a mixture.

00:02:32.165 --> 00:02:34.875
On the opposite side of the spectrum, people with fewer taste buds experience more muted flavors and smells

00:02:34.875 --> 00:02:38.035
And cannot distinguish between individual flavors in a mixture as easily.

00:02:38.035 --> 00:02:40.785
These different types of people are grouped into categories.

00:02:40.785 --> 00:02:43.115
Those with highly sensitive tongues are called supertasters

00:02:43.115 --> 00:02:45.525
And those with much less sensitive tongues are called non tasters, or sub-tasters

00:02:48.265 --> 00:02:50.855
That leaves the people in between with the label of middle tasters or medium tasters.

00:02:50.855 --> 00:02:53.765
Besides differences in flavor intensity, individuals also differ in the sensory capacities for the base tastes.

00:02:53.765 --> 00:02:56.655
Besides differences in flavor intensity, individuals also differ in the sensory capacities for the base tastes.

00:02:56.655 --> 00:02:59.325
This is due to structural differences of the receptors on your taste cells

00:03:01.375 --> 00:03:04.435
The structure of these receptors is purely genetics

00:03:04.435 --> 00:03:06.905
For example, there are around 30 different genes for bitterness perception,

00:03:06.905 --> 00:03:09.855
Which each register different levels of taste.

00:03:09.855 --> 00:03:12.285
With some not even registering the unpleasant bitterness at all.

00:03:12.285 --> 00:03:15.185
The second main contributing factor for taste perception

00:03:15.185 --> 00:03:18.235
Appears to be linked to where you live and your ethnic background

00:03:18.235 --> 00:03:20.975
For example, researchers used a bitter tasting chemical referred to as PTC

00:03:20.975 --> 00:03:23.915
To explore individual differences in taste sensitivity

00:03:23.915 --> 00:03:26.345
There are certain variants of a particular gene that renders some people unable to taste the chemical.

00:03:26.345 --> 00:03:27.725
There are certain variants of a particular gene that renders some people unable to taste the chemical.

00:03:27.725 --> 00:03:30.565
And research seems to indicate that there is a distinct difference in PTC sensitivity

00:03:30.565 --> 00:03:33.575
Between Africans and Non-Africans.

00:03:33.575 --> 00:03:36.695
In these charts, the blue represents the frequency of the gene variant which allows detection of PTC

00:03:36.695 --> 00:03:39.155
In these charts, the blue represents the frequency of the gene variant which allows detection of PTC

00:03:39.155 --> 00:03:41.965
Red shows the Non-Taster percentage,

00:03:41.965 --> 00:03:44.915
And grey represents the frequency of variants that confer intermediate taste sensitivity

00:03:44.915 --> 00:03:47.035
This map shows the populations with the lowest prevalence of taste sensitivity to PTC

00:03:47.035 --> 00:03:50.005
This map shows the populations with the lowest prevalence of taste sensitivity to PTC

00:03:50.005 --> 00:03:53.125
The darker the region, the higher the percentage of the population in the area that can taste the chemical

00:03:53.125 --> 00:03:55.905
With the exception of the aboriginal populations of Australia,

00:03:55.905 --> 00:03:57.575
The groups with the lowest prevalence of sensitivity to PTC

00:03:57.575 --> 00:04:00.585
Are located in Europe, the Middle East, and India

00:04:00.585 --> 00:04:03.560
Which has lead some researchers to believe that the genes associated with PTC non tasting

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:08.760
Were linked with populations that entered Europe from the middle east after the last ice age.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:13.800
As fascinating as all that is, I'm sure what you really want to know what kind of a taster you are.

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:18.500
Are you part of that special 25% of the population that has a heightened sense of taste?

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:20.920
Or the 25% that are considered non-tasters?

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:22.900
Or, are you just a regular middle taster?

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:25.160
There are some easy ways to determine the answer.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:28.080
If you're food preferences haven't already clued you in.

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:30.860
First, you can use blue food coloring to dye your tongue.

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:33.580
Fungiform Pappilae do not stain blue

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:35.800
so they stand out very clearly against your tongue.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:40.620
Without someone else's tongue to compare yours to, it might be difficult to tell if you're a non taster or a middle taster.

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:43.080
But it should be clear if you are a super taster.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:46.140
If you want something a little more scientific, you can buy a PTC Test Kit

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:48.640
Then if you're a supertaster, you'll know.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:52.000
But, if you can't  be bothered to dye your tongue or buy a test kit,

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:53.960
Here are some usual giveaways.

